Z||000000||pontiacs4me||04-19-2001||04:29 PM||||I dont know what has happened now, but my temp guage is reading about 460 degrees.  Its pegged all the way to the top right, pointing to about 2 oclock.  What makes this happen?  Also, sometimes my tach will just drop to zero, and then flop around like a fish between 0 and 1000 rpm.  What makes this happen. <BR>Im getting a vision, Im seeing autometers in the future.||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||04-19-2001||04:43 PM||||Give the top of your dash a nice smack and see if that returns the gauge to normal.<P>You could just replace the gauges. An inst. panel can probably be had from boneyard or EBAY for $15 or so.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bob Englert||04-19-2001||05:28 PM||||The temp gauge will peg when you start the car.  This is a problem with every Fiero.  You can often bang on the top of the dash to unstick it.  I did this on my blue GT until once when I banged on the dash and the tach needle fell off the gauge!<P>You should also check the connector at the sensor on the top of the head near the thermostat housing on the 4 cyl if I remember correctly.  This sensor will have 2 leads, one for the gauge and one for the warning light.  If the connector has come apart (common) and the lead for the gauge is shorting on something the needle will peg.  This is how you can test the circuit.  You can get replacement connectors from an auto parts store that sells AC Delco connectors.  My local AC Delco dealer had to hunt for it but it is available.<P>You might also want to check the main harness connectors to see that they are not corroded and are all making good contact.<P><BR>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl<p>[This message has been edited by Bob Englert (edited 04-19-2001).]||||reg||1
